  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   The proposed approach combines **structured initializations with iterative optimization** to systematically explore the solution space. The previous method relied on random initializations, which may not exploit structured properties of optimal solutions. By using **symmetric and periodic patterns**, we can leverage inherent regularity and reduce overlaps in integral overlaps. This approach addresses the bottleneck of limited exploration by combining deterministic structured patterns with numerical optimization. The symmetric patterns ensure balance across the interval [0,2], while periodic patterns distribute high/low regions evenly. This combination reduces the risk of local optima and improves the chances of finding a globally optimal solution.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   - **Representation**: The function $ h \\in [0,1]^n $, where $ n = n_{\\text{points}} $, and $ \\sum h_i = n/2 $.  \n   - **Objective**: Minimize $ C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x) (1-h(x + k)) dx $, equivalent to $ \\max(\\text{correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\"full\") \\cdot dx) $.  \n   - **Constraints**:  \n     1. $ 0 \\le h_i \\le 1 $.  \n     2. $ \\sum h_i = n/2 $.  \n   - **Structured Initial Guesses**:  \n     - **Symmetric Pattern**: $ h_{\\text{sym}}(x) = 0.5 (1 + \\cos(\\pi x)) $, ensuring symmetry around $ x=1 $.  \n     - **Periodic Pattern**: $ h_{\\text{period}}(x) = 0.5 (1 + \\cos(2\\pi x / T)) $, with $ T $ varying to test different spacings.  \n     - **Binary Pattern**: Alternating blocks of 1s and 0s of length $ L $, rescaled to meet the integral constraint.  \n   - **Solver**: SLSQP from scipy.optimize with equality constraint on the integral and bound constraints on $ h_i $.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   - **Input Handling**: If `initial_h_values` is provided, interpolate it to $ n $ points and normalize to $ \\sum h_i = n/2 $.  \n   - **Structured Initialization**:  \n     a. Generate symmetric, periodic, and binary patterns.  \n     b. For each, rescale to ensure $ \\sum h_i = n/2 $.  \n     c. Store these as initial guesses.  \n   - **Optimization Loop**:  \n     a. For each initial guess:  \n        i. Clip $ h_i \\in [0,1] $.  \n        ii. Run SLSQP with objective minimizing $ \\max(\\text{correlate}(h, 1-h) \\cdot dx) $, subject to:  \n           - Equality constraint $ \\sum h_i = n/2 $.  \n           - Bounds $ 0 \\le h_i \\le 1 $.  \n           - Tolerances $ ftol=1e-9 $, $ eps=1e-8 $, max iterations 300.  \n     b. Track the best candidate via $ C_5 $ value.  \n   - **Fallback**: If optimization fails, use the symmetric pattern as the default.  \n   - **Final Check**: Recompute $ C_5 $ for the best solution using the exact correlation method.  \n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \n   - **Initial Guess Diversity**:  \n     a. Vary $ T $ in periodic patterns (e.g., $ T = 2/(n/3) $, $ T=2/(n/4) $) to test spacing.  \n     b. For binary patterns, vary block length $ L $ (e.g., $ L= n/6 $, $ L= n/4 $) to test density.  \n   - **Optimization Parameters**:  \n     a. Adjust $ ftol $ to $ 1e-8 $ for tighter convergence.  \n     b. Use early stopping if $ C_5 $ improves by less than $ 1e-5 $ in 100 iterations.  \n   - **Variation Triggering**:  \n     a. If $ C_5 $ plateaus, try a different initial guess type (e.g., switch from periodic to binary).  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n   - **Time Estimation**: Each SLSQP run takes ~15s; 5 structured guesses fit within 600s (600s / 15s = 40 guesses).  \n   - **Deadline Checks**: Monitor elapsed time and terminate early if remaining time < 5s.  \n   - **Numerical Stability**:  \n     a. Clip values to $ [0,1] $ after each iteration.  \n     b. Rescale $ h $ to ensure $ \\sum h = n/2 $ after clipping.  \n   - **Fallback**: If all optimizations fail, return the symmetric pattern.  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n   - **Feasibility Checks**:  \n     a. Ensure $ 0 \\le h_i \\le 1 $.  \n     b. Verify $ \\sum h = n/2 $.  \n   - **Objective Consistency**: Recompute $ C_5 $ using the exact correlation method.  \n   - **Sanity Checks**:  \n     a. Ensure $ C_5 \\le 0.3807 $.  \n     b. Validate that no $ h_i $ exceeds the bounds.  \n   - **Return Format**: $ (h, C_5, n) $, ensuring all constraints are met.",
